A||||6||86SE||Front Swaybar bushings||||13||
Z||000000||86SE||06-27-2000||03:28 PM||||My front swaybar rubber bushings are about 14 years old.  It still handles excellent in my opinion, but im sure they are pretty much smashed down.<P>How much of a difference does polyurathane in handling if you poly bushings in just the 2 spots where the bar meets the chassis (the 2 main bushings)??||||reg||13
Z||000001||terryk||06-27-2000||04:30 PM||||Poly's are good and hard. They do help the handling, but they tend to squeek to the point of annoyance. I greased them but it only lasts a month or two. <P>I finally removed them and installed Moog K3110 in the clamps. You can tell they flex a bit more.<P>Many of the aftermarket replacement bushings are the wrong part.<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||06-27-2000||05:35 PM||||It's like adding a one size bigger sway bar.||||reg||1
Z||000003||lowCG||06-27-2000||08:29 PM||||Like a bigger bar,and better response,which is a different effect than just more bar.||||reg||1
Z||000004||86SE||06-27-2000||08:33 PM||||Alright sounds excellent!!  I'll pull the bushings and make some poly bushings and put em in tomorrow, weather permitting.  I'll try to design them so they dont squeek!  I've had enought squeeking this last week.......damn alt belt........||||reg||14
Z||000005||lowCG||06-27-2000||09:41 PM||||I found that a radial groove,in the center of the bushing is a good place to put grease.<BR>Just took and sank an average lathe tool in there about .200 or so.<BR>If you're going to up and make some,you might want to try some delrin,it'll wear WAY better than urethane,and it'll have less friction as well.A pre-load of around .005-.010 works great.I wish I had delrin in my rear control arm bushings like in my old car,was better than urethane any day;the car actually rode better with these,more than I can say about urethane.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||06-27-2000||09:44 PM||||I think the Energy Suspension brand sway bar bushings from Suspension Restoration Parts are graphite impregnated poly (not the Prothane control arm bushings). The bar bushings are $16 for the greasable bushings, which is a mere two bucks more than the regular ones. Get the greasable ones. The end link bushings cost $15 for the kit. So far, I have a couple thousand miles on my bushings I bought from them and no squeaking yet.<P> <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com/fiero-prothane.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.suspension.com/fiero-prothane.htm</A> ||||reg||1
